the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in R,$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n2\\cdot x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333508/field-4" "q-333508/field-5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-79894/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "b8f86089-bd44-5eb8-99c1-c07953e346df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79894/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "e8ae8c9a-ca0a-59e3-ad8e-d9ce74fb26e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79894/selection/a4d5bf0caab3ffc5e110cb0791b6afad9bab95b5658694add94464beae2e17af" :answer/id #uuid "46ca29e8-9cc6-53a3-8839-5ff18b4d1e65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79894/selection/8b8b19332750841a98f02901a999688c63f553a178c6f2ce2c643f8f06d5628e" :answer/id #uuid "96360958-733a-5859-91fe-ee5d1de85877" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79894/selection/2d6cfdfd554881720879744154242fb5aa6f8fe5d2ee6c2ca18d39657060f0e9" :answer/id #uuid "7530b915-306a-5d6f-b586-c9131951bfe4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79894/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c9ad8e7-371e-52b9-b37b-5218e73ee9cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79894/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-79894/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-79894/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-79894/selection/a4d5bf0caab3ffc5e110cb0791b6afad9bab95b5658694add94464beae2e17af" "q-79894/selection/8b8b19332750841a98f02901a999688c63f553a178c6f2ce2c643f8f06d5628e" "q-79894/selection/2d6cfdfd554881720879744154242fb5aa6f8fe5d2ee6c2ca18d39657060f0e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79894" :question/id #uuid "1835bf44-7892-5264-86c4-d1e16cd015d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79894" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=2x+1$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=2x+1$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x-1.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(2x+1){(x-1)}^{′}-(x-1){(2x+1)}^{′} \\\\  & =(2x+1)(1)-(x-1)(2) \\\\  & =2x+1-2x+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79894/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79894"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-129800/selection/fda3ea8de783244ca3676f7a3b5bf760b3fab3cd33a1c5dfba3958fe2da6bb1c" :answer/id #uuid "e7a2ee37-a2e9-53fe-bfc0-c49aea122023" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-129800/selection/b1900beeefcdfe1f5bf7bc8801728c6a5af3f9c31f6e2ffc6f69a702e7907a6b" :answer/id #uuid "f95f2625-b730-5ae8-930a-dd8ef9fab5df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-129800/selection/f40a2aa3dbeb568aa50f4238c5fa6969f278baae112465e4bfea0b545c0dbe83" :answer/id #uuid "465f78f2-e0bc-5d44-9b12-93c2e2723dda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-129800/selection/7470bd8542331dfac3fa223338c28ee57d853bad3b9099ef57e663cb2ef9e24e" :answer/id #uuid "356f5583-6bb9-53b8-a436-a67af12460eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-129800/selection/33fe0bbef045acd497bf1146e6be03cb640edd9a41c2946256441c074ddc4f9b" :answer/id #uuid "1370bcb4-379e-507a-9d30-0b5f3235fa65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-129800/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb5c8e1d-96db-5add-80ef-d0e2d08f4bf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-129800/selection/f40a2aa3dbeb568aa50f4238c5fa6969f278baae112465e4bfea0b545c0dbe83" :answer-field/choices ["q-129800/selection/fda3ea8de783244ca3676f7a3b5bf760b3fab3cd33a1c5dfba3958fe2da6bb1c" "q-129800/selection/b1900beeefcdfe1f5bf7bc8801728c6a5af3f9c31f6e2ffc6f69a702e7907a6b" "q-129800/selection/f40a2aa3dbeb568aa50f4238c5fa6969f278baae112465e4bfea0b545c0dbe83" "q-129800/selection/7470bd8542331dfac3fa223338c28ee57d853bad3b9099ef57e663cb2ef9e24e" "q-129800/selection/33fe0bbef045acd497bf1146e6be03cb640edd9a41c2946256441c074ddc4f9b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-129800" :question/id #uuid "c4006a71-c6e6-58f8-99f6-7e2943f5dd9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129800" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x){(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}-(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){(x)}^{′} \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-129800/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-129800"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-129797/selection/d36e5a05d5b5e04536a4916a55b1214e26df522de06f95aab619d0950aea715d" :answer/id #uuid "3a726b31-35cb-5780-af40-4f27527b6b18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-129797/selection/714268026ceb49a2be88b601f0de269be1684d8f9a9eec06ac23967236ff49b4" :answer/id #uuid "32e8ef85-954b-5ecd-beba-0fb0c65c2bf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answ