93" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({x}^{2}){({x}^{3})}^{′}-({x}^{3}){({x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79893/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79893"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149760 :answer-field/correct 17592186149761 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149757 :answer-field/correct 17592186149758 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149754 :answer-field/correct 17592186149756 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333517"] :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x+1},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x+1}-2{e}^{4x+1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334121/field-1/998925f26cdec7ec2a0c49e4ecc6eb78ccfc3a234931af7788cce51cb102f077" :answer/id #uuid "af86e475-8677-551c-bb21-bea1122a4c9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334121/field-1/20290c612f74c25a62a8c6938cf5d22ab000f382757142ddd721cda70773c9ef" :answer/id #uuid "fa73fedf-2d11-5dd4-8adc-e55c01ff7d8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334121/field-1/74521c50c65fc6a52056746481c6d39edacf577250ffbffd7ae5248b235295c6" :answer/id #uuid "ab8a013a-2ba4-5568-a44b-33101282badd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334121/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "d421c9f1-4d34-57a0-a155-17e5ee6a2231" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334121/field-1" :answer-field/id #uu